WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:07.200
There's one similarity between Amazon and Blue&nbsp;
Origin CEO Jeff Bezos, Apple's Tim Cook, Alphabet's &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:13.680
Sundar Pichai, AMD's Lisa Su, General Motors'&nbsp;
Mary Barra and NVIDIA's Jensen Huang. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.840
days, the heads of some of the most successful&nbsp;
companies in the world have one thing in common.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:24.000
They're engineers. For those who aspire to be&nbsp;
CEO of a company one day, getting an MBA - a Master&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.360
of Business Administration degree - used to be&nbsp;
the way to go. But that's not so true anymore.&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:35.920
Harvard Business Review used to publish an annual&nbsp;
list of the top 100 best-performing CEOs. In 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.480
they found that for a second year in a row, there&nbsp;
were more CEOs with an engineering degree than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:48.400
an MBA. 34 compared to 32. Some had both. Getting an&nbsp;
MBA can come with a hefty price tag. Top graduate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.960
schools such as the University of Pennsylvania's&nbsp;
Wharton or Harvard Business School will set you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.480
back over $200,000. Now,
some CEOs are telling you to save your money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:04.080
Elon Musk went so far as to say, "I think there&nbsp;
might be too many MBAs running companies" in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.480
interview with the Wall Street Journal. He thinks&nbsp;
those with business degrees spend too much time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:14.160
in board meetings pouring over power points and&nbsp;
finances when their focus should be on the product.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.520
The thinking being that if the product is good the&nbsp;
profits will follow. In the case of Musk that means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.120
building great electric cars at Tesla or great&nbsp;
reusable rockets at SpaceX. That's where being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:30.640
an engineer is helpful. Engineers can design, build, create, and improve their product because of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:36.000
background in math, science, and technology. And they&nbsp;
can help solve challenging technical problems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.360
might otherwise scuttle their dreams. Technically,&nbsp;
Musk has a degree in physics and economics not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.080
engineering. He's a self-taught engineer who&nbsp;
read books to learn about rocket science.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.920
When engineering consultant Sandy Munro
interviewed him for his YouTube channel&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:57.920
Munro Live, he said it's Musk's knowledge that&nbsp;
stands out. I was blown away. I've seen dozens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:06.560
of CEOs. I've never seen a CEO ever or a president&nbsp;
that knew more about the product. That technical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.360
know-how that comes with an engineering background&nbsp;
goes a long way toward building better products.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:17.040
Just ask Satya Nadella, the engineer&nbsp;
at Microsoft who rose to become CEO.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.560
When he took over the top job in 2014, he had to&nbsp;
figure out a way to make Microsoft relevant as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:29.040
faced an onslaught from Apple. We now need to make&nbsp;
Microsoft thrive. The software company struggled to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.760
thrive under the leadership of his predecessor&nbsp;
Steve Ballmer. Its products were far from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:39.600
revolutionary. The Surface was a response to the&nbsp;
iPad, the Windows phone and answer to the iPhone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.720
Ballmer has a degree in mathematics and economics&nbsp;
from Harvard which helped balance the books but

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:50.560
didn't do much when it comes to innovation. When&nbsp;
Nadella took over, he transformed Microsoft - moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.920
its software to non-Windows devices. For example,&nbsp;
bringing Microsoft Word to the iPad and the iPhone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.080
He also expanded its cloud business Azure which&nbsp;
has become the biggest rival to Amazon's AWS.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:08.720
In his first email to employees as CEO, Nadella&nbsp;
wrote: "Our industry does not respect tradition - it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:15.520
only respects innovation." That innovative spirit&nbsp;
of engineering saved Microsoft. But it's not easy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:21.600
It requires risky moves and not accepting failure.&nbsp;
As American inventor Thomas Edison famously said:&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:27.600
"I have not failed. I've only found 10,000 ways that&nbsp;
won't work." James Dyson, the British engineer, knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:32.320
a thing or two about not giving up. He became&nbsp;
frustrated when a vacuum he had at home kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:38.560
losing suction. So he decided to build a better&nbsp;
vacuum himself. He spent 15 years tinkering with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:44.080
over 5,000 different prototypes to come up with&nbsp;
the perfect bagless design. I'd like to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.800
you a little demonstration. The payoff for his&nbsp;
perseverance was a multi-billion dollar company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:55.200
that bears his name. In order to be successful,&nbsp;
Dyson was invested in the fine details of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.760
company. Engineers aren't afraid to get involved in&nbsp;
the day-to-day business - especially when it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:07.120
to hiring. Amazon is notorious for tough interviews.&nbsp;
Bezos, who has a degree in electrical engineering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:12.720
and computer science, used to meet every candidate&nbsp;
himself during Amazon's early days. He would ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:18.000
quirky questions like: How many gas stations are&nbsp;
there in America? He wasn't after the right answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.320
but wanted to see whether the candidate had an&nbsp;
analytical approach in order to come up with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:28.320
informed response. Bezos once said setting the bar&nbsp;
high for hiring was the most important factor for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.520
the online retailer's success. It's no surprise that&nbsp;
engineers make it to the top of the tech industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.800
which has seen explosive growth over the years.&nbsp;
But they're also doing well at non-tech companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.600
Like Jeffrey Sprecher, the CEO of the holding&nbsp;
company that owns the New York Stock Exchange.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.800
He has a degree in chemical engineering. I've never&nbsp;
had a job that had anything to do with chemistry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:54.240
but the discipline that I went through there&nbsp;
taught me about problem-solving and business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.520
is really just that. Engineers are very good at&nbsp;
solving problems and that's the key to doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.160
everything from building a bridge across a&nbsp;
river to designing a rocket to get to Mars.

